{
  "global_card_id": "equal_highs",
  "global_canonical": "Equal Highs",
  "type": "merged_concept",
  "school": "ICT",
  "aliases": [
    "Failure Swing Highs",
    "Stacked Highs",
    "Triple Top"
  ],
  "definition": "Two or more swing highs at approximately the same price level, forming a cluster of buy-side liquidity (resting buy stops from breakout traders and stop-losses of short sellers). This acts as a draw on liquidity that smart money is expected to sweep above the highs before a potential reversal or continuation in the prevailing direction.",
  "definition_per_source": {
    "Education - ICT (Updated)": "Two or more swing highs at approximately the same price level, forming a cluster of buy-side liquidity where resting buy stops accumulate. Price is expected to sweep these levels, acting as a draw-on-liquidity target, before reversing or continuing in the prevailing direction.",
    "SMC-Indicator-Supplement": "Two or more swing highs at approximately the same price level, forming a horizontal resistance zone where buy-side liquidity accumulates (short sellers' stop-losses and breakout traders' buy-stops). Smart money often targets this pool with a liquidity sweep above the highs, triggering stops before a potential downward reversal."
  },
  "identification_rules": [
    "Locate two or more swing highs (peaks with lower highs on both sides) at approximately the same horizontal price level, forming a tight cluster or horizontal resistance zone with minimal vertical separation.",
    "Confirm each high is a distinct, significant swing point separated by a visible decline or pullback — not a single impulsive move — to distinguish from minor fluctuations.",
    "Confirm the highs rest above current price action and represent a pool of buy-side liquidity (resting buy stops and short-seller stop-losses).",
    "Use a tolerance for minor price discrepancies when determining equality; SMC project recommends an ATR-based method, while ICT advises allowing a wider tolerance on instruments that frequently sweep slightly beyond the level (e.g., oil).",
    "SMC project: ensure the level functions as resistance, ideally aligning with a prior structural level (e.g., previous swing high).",
    "ICT project: verify that no higher time frame PD array (e.g., order block, fair value gap) sits directly overhead; if one does, classify the structure by that level instead.",
    "ICT project: an imbalance (fair value gap) above the equal highs strengthens the level as a draw on liquidity.",
    "ICT project: if highs are tightly stacked without meaningful separation, treat the upper-most high as the relevant swing high and the lower one(s) as failure swings; do not anticipate a reversal from a lower high before the upper high is swept.",
    "ICT project: apply a three-higher-time-frame-candle look-back period to determine relevance (e.g., 3 months on daily, 3 days on hourly charts).",
    "Monitor for price approach and a sweep of the level (liquidity grab); then look for displacement or reversal signals — ICT cites change in state of delivery or V-shaped reversal, SMC cites change of character or bearish fair value gap — after the sweep."
  ],
  "common_mistakes": [
    "Entering a trade (especially a short) before a confirmed liquidity sweep above the equal highs, or shorting directly into the level.",
    "Entering after the sweep without a confirmed reversal signal — ICT: change in state of delivery, displacement lower, candle 2/3 closure, or V-shaped reversal; SMC: change of character or bearish fair value gap.",
    "Targeting equal highs after price has already reached and reversed from them, rather than while price is still trading away from the level.",
    "Treating a break above equal highs as a genuine bullish breakout instead of a potential liquidity grab / stop hunt.",
    "Misidentifying equal highs by not applying appropriate tolerance (e.g., ATR-based) for price equality, leading to false signals.",
    "Ignoring the larger market structure and higher time frames (e.g., trend direction, opposing liquidity sweep, whether the highs are part of a larger liquidity structure).",
    "ICT project: trusting a reversal from a lower equal high before the upper (relevant) high is swept.",
    "ICT project: treating a series of up-close candles that formed equal highs as a valid order block when no higher time frame PD array sits above them.",
    "ICT project: using a stop loss that is too tight directly above the equal highs, especially in volatile instruments like oil where a wider sweep is common.",
    "ICT project: allowing price to retrace too deeply (e.g., into a lower fair value gap) before expecting expansion into equal highs, invalidating the bullish premise.",
    "ICT project: confusing single old swing highs with equal high clusters, which carry different liquidity implications.",
    "ICT project: using equal highs that fall outside the three-higher-time-frame-candle look-back period, assuming they remain relevant."
  ],
  "trading_implication": "Equal highs act as a buy-side liquidity pool and price magnet (short-seller stops plus breakout buy-stops) that smart money is expected to sweep. ICT frames them dually: as profit targets in bullish scenarios (a draw on liquidity) or as the manipulation leg before a bearish reversal; entries come after the sweep, confirmed by change in state of delivery, displacement lower, or a V-shaped/candle 2-3 closure, with stop losses set to account for the sweep (wider in assets like oil). SMC focuses on the bearish case: in a downtrend or bearish bias, smart money breaks above to trap buyers, then reverses; traders wait for the sweep plus a bearish confirmation (change of character, bearish fair value gap, or rejection at a supply zone) before going short. Both schools agree: never short directly into equal highs — wait for the sweep and directional confirmation.",
  "merge_notes": "Both sources share the core liquidity-pool concept, but differ in scope and emphasis. ICT treats equal highs bidirectionally — as a bullish draw-on-liquidity target as well as a pre-reversal manipulation leg — and adds richer contextual filters (HTF PD arrays, three-candle look-back, FVG confluence, failure-swing handling). SMC frames them primarily as a bearish resistance/stop-hunt setup and contributes an ATR-based tolerance method for measuring equality. Confirmation vocabulary differs (ICT: change in state of delivery / V-shaped reversal; SMC: change of character / bearish FVG) though both require post-sweep confirmation.",
